namespace BrineBattle.Hunting
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The full guide, shown only in Settings → FAQ (Ed's call: no guide text on the play screen or in
    /// the other menus). Plain text so the player manual (U-22) can reuse it later.
    /// </summary>
    public static class Faq
    {
        public struct Entry
        {
            public string Question, Answer;
            public Entry(string q, string a) { Question = q; Answer = a; }
        }

        public struct Topic
        {
            public string Name;
            public Entry[] Entries;
            public Topic(string name, params Entry[] entries) { Name = name; Entries = entries; }
        }

        public static readonly Topic[] Topics =
        {
            new Topic("Start",
                new Entry("What do I do in this game?",
                    "You live on a raft on an endless ocean. Monsters and loot float past. Hook them with your harpoon, fight or harvest them, then use the loot to build a bigger, stronger raft, make gear and potions, and raise minions."),
                new Entry("What are the keys?",
                    "Click a floating thing: throw the harpoon at it.\nSpace: reel in.\nB: Bag.  P: Minions.  J: Quests.  C: Journal.  R: Build.  Esc: Settings, or close a menu.\nMouse wheel or + / -: zoom in and out.  Right-drag or Q / E: turn the view.  F: put the view back.\nYou can also click the picture buttons at the bottom of the screen."),
                new Entry("What do the boxes at the top-left mean?",
                    "Hunter: your HP and any ailment. Raft: its level, HP, size and type. Harpoon: how worn it is (press Fix to mend it). Minions: each one's level, EXP and what it is doing. The line under them shows the day, the time, the weather and the forecast (what weather comes next, and when).")),

            new Topic("Hunting",
                new Entry("How do I catch something?",
                    "Click a monster or item floating on the water. The harpoon flies out. When it hooks, a tall reel bar opens: the catch darts up and down, and you hold or tap Space (or the mouse) to lift the green zone and keep the catch inside it. Inside, the meter fills; outside, it drains. Full meter = caught, empty = it slips off the hook. Rarer and bigger catches have a narrower green zone and move wilder; Alchemy Mastery and a Looter minion widen it. Catching it while barely leaving the zone is a clean reel: extra loot, and you strike first."),
                new Entry("What do \"Looks dead\", \"Twitching...\" and \"Something moves!\" mean?",
                    "It's a guess about the monster's state, and it can be wrong.\nLooks dead: usually safe to harvest for loot.\nTwitching...: weak; finish it for loot, or tame it as a minion.\nSomething moves!: alive; it may fight you."),
                new Entry("What are Harvest, Finish it, Tame, Fight, Flee and Cut line?",
                    "Harvest: take the loot from a dead monster.\nFinish it: beat a weak monster for its loot.\nTame: try to make a weak, calm monster your minion.\nFight!: start a battle.\nCut line: let it go, nothing lost.\nFlee: run from it, but it bites off one raft part on the way (see Raft).\nGreen buttons are the safe choice, red is risky, blue lets it go."),
                new Entry("What is floating loot?",
                    "Crates, bundles and wrecks that float past (\"Floating loot\" over them). Hook one to get raft materials like planks. Raft modules catch floating loot of their own rarity or lower, and a Looter minion can fetch it too."),
                new Entry("Can a monster hurt me outside a battle?",
                    "Yes. A live monster may lash out while you reel it in, and a monster you just beat may thrash one last time. Either can hit you or a minion, and may leave an ailment. It never knocks you out, but it can knock out a pet."),
                new Entry("Some monsters are too big. Why?",
                    "A small raft can't fight a big monster. Grow the raft (more parts) to take on bigger ones.")),

            new Topic("Day & weather",
                new Entry("Is there day and night?",
                    "Yes. A full day takes 8 minutes: dawn, day, dusk and night. Time stops while a menu is open or a battle is on."),
                new Entry("What are Sun and Moon creatures?",
                    "Fire, magma, sand and steam creatures are Sun creatures: by day they hit 20% harder and take a bit less damage, at night they hit 10% softer. Water, ice and mud creatures are Moon creatures, the other way round. Earth, wind and lightning are neither. At dawn and dusk nobody is boosted.\nThis counts for monsters, your minions, and you: gear with a sun or moon element powers you up at its time. Sun creatures turn up more by day, Moon creatures more at night."),
                new Entry("What changes at night?",
                    "More drifts are alive, hints are wrong a bit more often, and you can only read the names of drifts close to the raft. Far ones show as \"???\"."),
                new Entry("What does each weather do?",
                    "Clear: calm, and on clear days more drifts are dead (easy loot).\nRain: water creatures hit harder, hints a bit worse.\nRough sea: big waves, drifts float by faster, your harpoon throw is 2 m shorter and the reel's sweet spot is smaller. More creatures are alive.\nStorm: all of that, worse. Lightning creatures hit harder, lots of floating loot, and lightning can strike the raft.\nFog: flat sea, but you can only read drifts close by and hints are often wrong."),
                new Entry("Lightning hit my raft. What do I do?",
                    "A part is on fire. Click the fire twice to throw water on it. You can't hook anything until it's out. If it burns for 15 seconds, that part is lost. A Crewmate minion puts fires out by itself after 5 seconds. Sea-level ships get hit half as often; Aerial ships stand tall and get hit more."),
                new Entry("What is the forecast for?",
                    "It tells you the next weather and when it comes. A storm only grows out of rain or a rough sea, so you get a warning: heal up, get a Crewmate minion, or reel in what you can before it hits."),
                new Entry("Why is there so much floating loot after a storm?",
                    "When a storm clears, the next 3 drifts are wreckage: floating loot one rarity higher than usual.")),
            new Topic("Battle",
                new Entry("How do battles work?",
                    "Turns: you, your battle minion and the monster each act by speed. Attack, use a skill or an item. Elements matter: a strong element hits harder."),
                new Entry("What are Manual, Semi-auto and Auto?",
                    "Manual: you pick every action and play the reel minigame yourself.\nSemi-auto: basic attacks are automatic; you decide the important turns, and the reel's green zone helps follow the catch.\nAuto: the game plays every turn and plays the reel minigame for you.\nChange it in Settings → Game, or during a battle."),
                new Entry("What is auto-use?",
                    "In Semi-auto and Auto, potions are used for you when HP is low. Turn it on or off in Settings → Game."),
                new Entry("What are ailments?",
                    "Some monsters' hits leave an ailment. The sign over a drift tells you which one:\n☠ Poison: lose a little HP every round.\n♦ Bleed: lose more HP every round, but it ends sooner.\n? Confusion: sometimes you hit yourself.\n◉ Blind: you often miss and can't use gear skills.\nAilments stay after a battle and keep ticking every few seconds, but outside battle they never take your last HP."),
                new Entry("How do I cure ailments and heal?",
                    "Workbench → Player → Potions: Toxic Relief (poison), Clotting Patch (bleeding), Confusion Relief, Eye Cleanser (blind), Brine Tonic and Big Brine Tonic (HP). Minion treats like the Kelp Biscuit are in Workbench → Minion. In battle use them from the item buttons (Semi-auto and Auto use them for you). On the raft: Bag → Use for yourself, Minions → pick one for your minions. You and your minions also heal slowly on the raft."),
                new Entry("What does \"⚠ Danger!\" over a monster mean?",
                    "That monster would probably beat you and your battle minion. Better gear, a stronger minion, or leaving it alone helps."),
                new Entry("What happens if I lose?",
                    "The monster swims off and you get no loot. It also eats one raft part from the edge of your raft (see Raft → Gorged monsters). You rest for 30 seconds before you can hook again, and the waves wash away up to 3 Common materials from your Bag. Gear, minions, tools, potions and rarer items in the Bag are never lost. Your minions hide in the storage box until you're back up. After the rest you're back to full HP with no ailments. Losing a fight a module started doesn't cost a raft part.")),

            new Topic("Bag & loot",
                new Entry("Where is my loot?",
                    "In the Bag (B). Use the filter buttons (Materials, Raft, Gear, Potions, Tools) to find things fast."),
                new Entry("What is Salvage and Monster Essence?",
                    "Salvage breaks an item into Monster Essence. Rarer items give more. Essence pays for raft level-ups, gear refining and minion fusing."),
                new Entry("What is auto-salvage?",
                    "Bag → Auto-salvage. Weapons and armor below the tiers you set there are \"misfits\". If auto-salvage is on, misfits are turned into essence when you pick them up. Every other kind of loot is always kept, at any raft level."),
                new Entry("How do I equip gear?",
                    "Bag → Equipment. Press Equip on a weapon, armor or accessory. Refine it with essence to make it stronger. Rarer gear has skills."),
                new Entry("What are accessories?",
                    "A third gear slot (Workbench → Gear → Accessory). An accessory adds a little attack and defense (more with each +), and its worth is its skills: rarer ones have more (Rare 1 passive, Epic 1 passive + 1 active, Legendary 2 + 2). Its element counts when your weapon has none, so a Water accessory makes you stronger at night and a Fire one by day. Swift gives +2 speed."),
                new Entry("Where did Dismantle go?",
                    "To the Salvage Bin, the metal bin next to the storage box. Click it (or Bag → Essence → Open the Salvage Bin, or Workbench → Dismantle). Everything you salvage or take apart, from the Bag or the Build menu, flies into the bin as glowing essence. Open it to see the essence inside and to take things apart.")),

            new Topic("Mastery",
                new Entry("What is Alchemy Mastery?",
                    "Your skill as a monster alchemist, shown at the top-left. Harvesting or beating a monster gives 2 points, taming 3, crafting 1. Each level needs more points (10, 20, 30 …)."),
                new Entry("What does Mastery do?",
                    "• Hints over drifts (\"Looks dead\" etc.) are right more often: 60% at Lv 1, up to 96%.\n• New recipes appear: Uncommon things at Lv 2, Rare at Lv 3, Epic at Lv 4, Legendary at Lv 5, Mythic at Lv 6. Until then they show as ??? in the Workbench and the Journal.\n• From Lv 3 you can combine 5 essence into 1 of the next rarity (Bag → Essence)."),
                new Entry("Why is a recipe \"???\"?",
                    "Your Alchemy Mastery isn't high enough yet. The card says which level reveals it. Hunt, harvest and craft to level up. Book → Recipes lists every recipe you know.")),

            new Topic("Workbench",
                new Entry("Where do I craft?",
                    "Build (R) → Workbench. Pick a tab, then a small tab under it: Gear (Weapon, Armor, Accessory), Player (Potions, Oils), Minion (Treats, Habitats), Raft (Planks & parts, Auto-loot, Hammers). Taking things apart is in the Salvage Bin."),
                new Entry("How do I know what I'm missing?",
                    "Every recipe card lists each ingredient as have/need. Green ✔ = enough. Red ✘ = not enough, and it says how many more. Recipes you can make now are at the top, and each tab shows how many are ready, like \"Raft (2)\". Tick \"Show only what I can make now\" to hide the rest."),
                new Entry("What do the tabs make?",
                    "Gear: weapons, armor and accessories.\nPlayer: potions that heal you or fix the raft, and oils that coat your weapon with an element.\nMinion: treats for your minions (Kelp Biscuit) and habitats where they live.\nRaft: planks and stronger parts (Reinforce), auto-loot modules, and hammers."),
                new Entry("What do minions do here?",
                    "An Alchemist sometimes makes one extra gear, potion or oil. A Smith crafts or upgrades your hammer by itself.")),

            new Topic("Raft",
                new Entry("How do I build?",
                    "In the Shipyard: press Y (or Build → Open the Shipyard). Your raft or ship stands alone on a dock. Drag, WASD or the arrow keys turn it left, right, up and down; the mouse wheel zooms; Reset view puts it back. Pick a part on the left, hover a cell to see what a click does, click to place it (or swap a built part), right-click to take a part off; it goes back to the Bag. The game is paused while you're there, and minions don't help. Press Done, Esc or Y to go back."),
                new Entry("What are shaped modules?",
                    "Bigger modules: 1×2 (raft Lv 10), 2×2 (Lv 20), L (Lv 30), T (Lv 40) and 1×3 (on a ship). A kit lays several cells of its module at once; press R in the Shipyard to turn it, and hover to see its footprint. Kits are alchemy: a Builder or Alchemist minion makes them (Workbench → Raft → Auto-loot), from one module per cell plus a fang. Taking the cells off later gives back plain modules."),
                new Entry("What are blueprints?",
                    "Shipyard → Blueprints. Save your layout with a name; Load one to see it as a ghost; Build from bag places what you have and the rest becomes a quest (your Builder makes those parts first). Copy code gives a text code to share anywhere (for example Discord); Paste a share code adds someone else's design to your list. A copy keeps the original author's credit, and copying a design never gives you its parts."),
                new Entry("Why can't I build?",
                    "You need a hammer of the raft's level or better. Make or upgrade it in Workbench → Raft → Hammers. A Smith minion makes it for you too."),
                new Entry("How do I start the raft over?",
                    "Settings → Game → Reset raft. The raft goes back to the starter 3x3 so you can build it again; its level and type stay. About 1 in 5 of the parts that come off float away (\"Your lost raft part!\" over them): hook them, or let a module or Looter minion catch them, before the current takes them. The rest go to the Bag. Habitats are never lost."),
                new Entry("Why is a part \"not for\" my ship?",
                    "It belongs to a ship line you didn't pick. Once you pick a line, those parts come off by themselves, wait in the Bag and can't be placed again."),
                new Entry("Why is a part \"too weak\"?",
                    "After a level-up the raft only takes parts of its level or higher. Turn old parts into stronger ones with the Reinforce recipes in Workbench → Raft → Planks & parts."),
                new Entry("How do I level up the raft?",
                    "Build → Raft tree. The raft goes from Lv 1 to Lv 50, a little at a time. Every step shows ✔ when done and ✘ when not:\n• beat monsters (2 a level at first, more at higher tiers)\n• monster materials in the Bag\n• Monster Essence of the raft's tier or better\n• auto-loot modules on half the rows of the left edge\n• a hammer of the raft's tier\n• at the end of each tier (Lv 16, 33 and 50) its boss\n• on a ship: a ship part at Lv 20, 40, 60, 80 and 99.\nWhen all are ✔, press the green Level up button. The tier goes up every few levels (raft Lv 17 Uncommon, 34 Rare; ship Lv 1 Epic, 34 Legendary, 67 Mythic): then the hammer and parts must be that tier too."),
                new Entry("What are modules?",
                    "Nets and hooks that grab loot floating into them, even monsters (then a battle starts by itself). Put them on the left edge, where drifts come from. They wear out: repair them in Build → Modules, or let a Crew pet do it."),
                new Entry("Which module should I build?",
                    "Each module catches floating loot up to its own rarity: Drift Net and Hook Arm (Common), Tarred Net (Uncommon), Shell Trap (Rare), Coral Snare (Epic), Leviathan Grapple (Legendary), Abyssal Maw (Mythic). Each one upgrades from the one before in Workbench → Raft → Auto-loot. A Crewmate minion walks over and hammers each new part or module into place, with a progress bar; it takes a few seconds, never instantly. Monsters are caught by any module."),
                new Entry("What are ship lines?",
                    "At raft Lv 50 your raft is a simple boat, and you pick its ship line, once:\n• Sea level, STR: tough and strong (more raft HP, ATK, DEF). Sloop → Brigantine → Frigate → Galleon → Flagship.\n• Aerial, INT: reaches far (longer hook reach, ATK, SPD). Glider → Balloon Skiff → Airship → Sky Cruiser → Sky Citadel.\n• Undersea, DEX: the fastest (big SPD). Diving Bell → Mini-sub → Submarine → Deep Cruiser → Abyss Ark.\nThe ship starts again at Lv 1 and evolves up to Lv 100; its bonus grows with its level. After Lv 100 comes the next evolution: coming soon."),
                new Entry("How do I make ship parts and materials?",
                    "After the pick, the ship line's branch opens in Build → Raft tree, one tier at a time: its tree materials and typed parts (Workbench → Raft). Ship parts (Workbench → Raft → Vehicle) take two typed parts and a boss core."),
                new Entry("What is the raft tree?",
                    "Build → Raft tree: the evolution road, what the next level needs with the Level up button, the ship pick at raft Lv 50, the ship's parts, and the ship line's branch."),
                new Entry("What is a gorged monster?",
                    "When you lose a fight or flee, the monster eats one raft part from the edge (never one that would split the raft, and never when the raft has 5 parts or fewer). It swims off gorged: later it comes back with \"Gorged!\" over it, 20% stronger for each part it ate. Beat it to get every part back in your Bag, plus one extra loot roll per part. Build shows how many parts gorged monsters are holding."),
                new Entry("What are habitats?",
                    "Tide Pool and Sky Perch. Each one on the raft gives room for one more minion, and makes minions heal faster.")),

            new Topic("Bosses & fame",
                new Entry("What are bosses?",
                    "The end of each tier has a boss: raft Lv 16, 33 and 50, ship Lv 33, 66 and 99. It comes once you've beaten enough monsters for the next level, and it's big, always alive and can't be tamed. Beat it to go on. Its core is needed for a ship part. If you lose or let it pass, it comes back. You can also summon it from the Quest tab."),
                new Entry("What happens in a boss fight?",
                    "At half HP the boss becomes ENRAGED: it hits harder and sends a tidal wave at the raft every 3 rounds (Shell Guard stops one). All your minions help: other Fighters strike too, Alchemists heal, Crewmates patch the raft, and each Looter gives one more roll of the boss's treasure (up to 3). The treasure is its core plus a pile of its essence."),
                new Entry("What are ship parts?",
                    "Five per ship line: Sea level (Hull Walls, Cannons, Bow & Bowsprit, Masts & Sails, Stern Castle), Aerial (Wings, Tail Fin, Propeller, Engine, Cockpit), Undersea (Pressure Hull, Ballast Tanks, Screw Propeller, Periscope, Sonar Dome). They're needed at ship Lv 20, 40, 60, 80 and 99. Make them at Workbench → Raft → Vehicle, then put them on in Build → Raft tree. Each one shows on the ship."),
                new Entry("What is the goal?",
                    "Fame. Every monster, boss, tame, craft and new best raft or ship level gives fame, and achievements give more (Quest tab → Fame). Your rank grows with it: Castaway, Deckhand, Sailor, Captain, Famed Captain, Legend of the Brine."),
                new Entry("What do the difficulties change?",
                    "Picked when you start a new game. Easy: monsters ×1, loot ×1, fame ×1. Normal: monsters ×1.25, loot ×0.8, fame ×1.5. Hard: monsters ×1.5, loot ×0.6, fame ×2. Bosses always drop their core.")),

            new Topic("Minions",
                new Entry("How do I get a minion?",
                    "Hook a monster that is \"Twitching...\" and calm, then press Tame. Rarer monsters are harder to tame. You need room: build habitats for more."),
                new Entry("How do minions grow?",
                    "Minions → pick one → Feed. Loot gives EXP: gear gives the most, then raft parts, modules and hammers, then materials; rarer loot gives more, and its favourite element counts double. Potions add stats instead. Every level adds HP and ATK or DEF. At Lv 10 it evolves into an Adult, at Lv 20 into an Elder."),
                new Entry("How do minions learn skills?",
                    "Every minion starts with Fighter. New skills come at Lv 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 22, 26 and 30, as long as it has room: 2 + its rarity + its stage (a Common Young holds 2, a Mythic Elder 9). Its element decides the next two skills, then each kind of monster has its own order. Minions → Skill tree shows what's learned, what's next and at which level, and what's locked."),
                new Entry("What do the skills do?",
                    "Battle: Fighter fights beside you; Hardy (+HP, +DEF, Adult+); Ferocious (+ATK, +SPD, Elder).\nCrew: Crewmate installs modules and builds parts, patches the raft in battle and puts out fires; Mender mends the harpoon and modules; Builder (Uncommon+) crafts raft parts, modules and vehicle parts, puts vehicle parts on and levels the raft or ship up when everything is ready (the ship pick stays yours).\nAlchemy: Alchemist keeps potions, oils and treats stocked and heals in battle; Smith refines your gear and makes the next hammer; Tinker (Rare+) crafts better gear and equips it.\nLooter: Looter fetches dead drifts and floating loot; Salvager takes junk apart; Glutton eats the rest of the junk.\nEach job is done by the strongest minion that has the skill. Switch jobs on or off in Minions → Chores."),
                new Entry("What is junk?",
                    "Weapons and armor below their tier (Bag → Auto-salvage), accessories, raft parts and modules below the raft's level, and hammers older than your best one. Never materials or potions, and never parts a recipe still needs. Junk at or under the rarity in Minions → Chores → Salvager is dismantled (or salvaged into essence); rarer junk is eaten for EXP."),
                new Entry("Do minions get hurt?",
                    "They have health: hits in battle, a monster lashing out, or a last thrash after a fight can hurt them or give them an ailment. A minion at 0 HP is knocked out: it rests and doesn't work until it heals. Minions heal slowly on the raft, faster with each Tide Pool or Sky Perch. A Kelp Biscuit heals one right away."),
                new Entry("What is Fuse?",
                    "Minions → Fuse puts one minion into another. Same kind: it becomes rarer. Different kinds: a hybrid. It keeps the higher level and gets one skill from the other. The used minion is gone, and it can fail. Essence as a catalyst makes it safer."),
                new Entry("What is Look?",
                    "A costume: Plain, Scarf, Bandana or Crown. It's just for looks.")),

            new Topic("Journal & saving",
                new Entry("What is the Journal?",
                    "C opens it. Monsters, items, minions and recipes you've found (??? means not found yet; each tab shows found/total), and the Music tab, where you can play each piece of the soundtrack on its own."),
                new Entry("How do I save?",
                    "Settings → Save → Save now. There is one save file. In Semi-auto and Auto it also saves by itself every 5 minutes once a save exists. The top-right corner shows when."),
                new Entry("What if I fall asleep while playing?",
                    "If you don't press anything for 10 minutes, the game pauses by itself and shows \"You fell asleep\". Nothing fights, gets hooked or gets lost while you're away. In Semi-auto and Auto it saves first. A battle that is already going finishes first. If you lose a battle while away (no input for a minute), it pauses right away, without waiting for the timer. A loss costs a short rest and a few Common materials (see Battle). Press any key or click to continue. Change the time (or turn it off) in Settings → Game → Pause when I'm away."),
                new Entry("How do I start over?",
                    "Settings → Save → Save and go to the main menu, then New game. Your old save is kept as a backup.")),
        };
    }
}
